Quoting  Ray  <bear@sonic.net>:
>FWIW, I'm going to quote Wikipedia's entry on nethack to you. The second
>paragraph reads:
>The "net" element references that its development has been coordinated
>through the Internet.[4] The "hack" element refers to a genre of
>role-playing games known as hack and slash for their focus on combat. The
>player takes the part of a dungeon-delving character in search of the
>Amulet of Yendor.[5]
>Notice a couple of things.  First, they identify it as a "role-playing 
>game," implying that they're using a definition which includes nethack.

As indeed the "stats and levels" definition does.

>Second, they say what role the player actually plays - so clearly they 
>think that nethack is a game where you play a role, too. 

However, what answer you get from Wikipedia depends on where you look.
Wikipedia "Role-playing game" is about actual roleplaying games, and
mentions computer games as a separate second definition - distinct from
games where you "play the role of a character", that phrase being used
explicitly only about tabletop roleplaying.

>I consider this an example of majority usage.

I'll say it again; "stats and levels" is a common usage, albeit a silly
one and one that occasionally does actual damage. "Play a role" is a
perhaps less common usage. But this hybrid usage where you rationalise
something about "identifying with the @" but really it turns out to mean
stats-and-levels game is a minority compared with either of the first two
usages.
